    rationale: `This tests understanding of TURP SYNDROME, a specific complication of transurethral procedures. Clinical reasoning: (1) TURP procedure: Prostate resected via endoscope through urethra. Requires continuous irrigation to maintain visualization and cooling. (2) Irrigation fluids: Must be non-electrolyte (isotonic saline causes electrical conductivity issues). Options: Glycine (1.5%), water, sorbitol. Glycine is common. (3) Problem: During prolonged TURP (>60-90 minutes), large volume of hypotonic irrigant can be systemically ABSORBED through opened venous sinuses in the prostate. Absorption causes: (a) Hypervolemia (fluid overload). (b) Hyponatremia (dilution). (c) Neurologic effects from glycine (hyperammonemia, visual disturbances from glycine toxicity). (4) Clinical presentation (classic triad + more): (a) Hypertension (early, from fluid overload). (b) Bradycardia (from elevated intracranial pressure—Cushing triad: hypertension, bradycardia, irregular respirations). (c) Confusion, restlessness (from hyponatremia and hyperammonemia). (d) Visual disturbances (glycine can cause transient blindness). (e) Seizures can develop. (f) Cerebral edema, coma, death if severe. (5) Timing: Usually develops during or shortly after procedure if >60 minutes. (6) Risk factors: Prolonged TURP, capsular perforation (worse absorption), large gland resection. (7) Prevention: Limit operative time to <60 minutes if possible. Monitor fluid balance. Use bipolar TURP with normal saline (safer if available). (8) Treatment: (a) Stop procedure immediately. (b) Fluid restriction. (c) Furosemide diuretic. (d) Hypertonic saline (3%) if severe hyponatremia/seizures. (e) Seizure precautions. (f) ICU monitoring. (9) Study guide mentions electrolyte imbalances and seizures but doesn't specifically detail TURP syndrome (somewhat advanced topic). However, the pathophysiology aligns with hyponatremia concepts. Option B is correct and shows understanding of TURP syndrome's unique presentation and pathophysiology.`,
