    rationale: `This tests understanding of FAT EMBOLISM and its acute cardiopulmonary consequences. Clinical reasoning: (1) Fat embolism occurs when: Long bone (femur, tibia) is fractured or surgically manipulated → bone marrow fat is released into venous circulation. (2) This patient: Bilateral knee replacements involve cutting through bone. Marrow fat can enter venous sinuses. (3) Fat globules travel through venous system → right heart → lungs. In lungs: (a) Emboli lodge in pulmonary vessels. (b) Cause acute pulmonary hypertension and right heart strain. (c) Impair gas exchange. (d) Trigger inflammation and platelet aggregation (coagulopathy). (4) Classic presentation (can be acute or delayed hours): (a) RESPIRATORY: Hypoxia, dyspnea, tachypnea. (b) CARDIOVASCULAR: Hypotension, tachycardia, chest pain (right heart strain). (c) NEUROLOGIC: Confusion (from hypoxia, emboli to brain). (d) SKIN: Petechiae (nonblanching rash from capillary damage). (e) COAGULOPATHY: Thrombocytopenia, prolonged PT. (5) This patient shows acute respiratory distress + hemodynamic instability = fat embolism until proven otherwise. (6) Management: (a) OXYGENATION FIRST: High-flow O2 (100% FiO2), ensure mechanical ventilation is adequate, consider intubation if not already (she is under GA). (b) Hemodynamic support: IV fluids, consider vasopressors if BP remains low. (c) STOP SURGERY: Don't continue bilateral knee surgery in patient with acute cardiopulmonary crisis. Second knee can wait. (d) ICU-level monitoring. (e) Coagulation studies (PT, PTT, fibrinogen, D-dimer). (f) Positional: Head-of-bed elevated to support oxygenation (NOT flat/supine as option D suggests). (g) Anticoagulation controversial (heparin might be considered but increases bleeding risk). (7) Prevention: Careful surgical technique, venting of bone canal, gentle handling of limb. (8) Prognosis: Most recover, but severe cases can have ARDS, death. (9) Study guide mentions fat embolism briefly. This teaches the importance of recognizing acute changes intraoperatively. Option B is correct and prioritizes immediate oxygenation, hemodynamic support, and surgical pause.`,
