    rationale: `This tests understanding of POSITION-RELATED COMPLICATIONS during prolonged surgery and COMPARTMENT SYNDROME recognition. Clinical reasoning: (1) Lithotomy position (legs in stirrups, hips and knees flexed, external rotation): Commonly used for pelvic/GU procedures. Risks include: Femoral nerve compression, calf compartment syndrome, rhabdomyolysis. (2) Complications increase with: Duration (this patient 3.5 hours is long), inadequate padding, excessive hip flexion. (3) Signs of compartment syndrome: (a) PAIN (severe, out of proportion to exam). (b) Swelling (tissue edema from increased compartment pressure). (c) Discoloration (mottled from vascular compromise). (d) Diminished pulses (from vessel compression). (e) Paresthesias (nerve compression—would see if patient awake). (4) Compartment syndrome is a surgical EMERGENCY: Compartmental pressure builds → vessels compressed → tissue ischemia → muscle death → myoglobinuria → rhabdomyolysis → acute kidney injury (as discussed in earlier topics). (5) Fasciotomy (cutting fascia to release pressure) is the definitive treatment. Timing is critical—delay >6-8 hours risks permanent damage. (6) Venous thromboembolism (option A) is also a concern post-op but wouldn't cause THIS acute presentation with diminished pulses and swelling that appears immediately upon repositioning. (7) Normal post-op pain (option C) wouldn't present with mottled skin and pulse changes. (8) Management: (a) Notify surgical/anesthesia team immediately. (b) Measure compartment pressures (normal <20 mmHg, at-risk 20-30, critical >30 mmHg). (c) If compartment pressures elevated, urgent orthopedic consultation for possible fasciotomy. (d) Monitor urine color (myoglobinuria = dark/cola-colored). (e) Aggressive IV hydration to prevent rhabdomyolysis-induced AKI. (9) Study guide emphasizes position-related complications as a key intra-op concern. Option B is correct and identifies the urgent compartment syndrome risk.`,
