    q: `A 71-year-old female undergoing spinal fusion surgery is placed in PRONE position (face-down on operating table) for 4 hours. The patient is under general anesthesia with endotracheal tube. At the end of surgery, as the patient is being repositioned to supine, the anesthesiologist notes: FACIAL EDEMA, difficulty re-intubating/visualizing vocal cords, and LOSS OF BREATH SOUNDS bilaterally. The presumed diagnosis is:`,
    a: "Prone position airway edema from dependent head positioning (gravity pulled fluid to face/neck) combined with potential pressure on neck structures; airway is compromised post-operative requiring urgent management—may need emergency tracheostomy if can't intubate safely",
