    rationale: `This tests understanding of MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA EARLY RECOGNITION and the critical concept that FEVER IS LATE. Clinical reasoning: (1) This patient has CLASSIC risk: Family history of anesthetic complications (father ICU stay, uncle complications). (2) Intra-operative findings: (a) Tachycardia—early sign of MH. (b) Tachypnea—hypermetabolism from MH. (c) Masseter rigidity (jaw clenching = trismus)—EARLY sign of muscle rigidity. (d) RISING ETCO2 (68 mmHg, normal 35-45)—indicates hypermetabolism. (3) Temperature: Patient NOT febrile YET, but fever is a LATE sign. Study guide emphasizes: "LATE SIGN: Fever is a LATE sign! Do NOT wait for fever to suspect MH!" The patient's other signs are PRESENT—fever will follow if MH is not treated. (4) Trigger agents: Sevoflurane (inhalational anesthetic) and succinylcholine are BOTH known triggers of MH. This patient received both—HIGH RISK situation. (5) IMMEDIATE ACTION (STOP-MH mnemonic): (a) STOP anesthesia—discontinue sevoflurane and succinylcholine. (b) Tell team—notify immediately that MH is suspected. (c) Oxygen 100%—high-flow O2. (d) Prepare dantrolene—have it ready (mixing takes time). (e) Monitor vitals/labs closely. (f) Hyperventilate—blow off CO2. (6) Dantrolene: 2.5 mg/kg IV, repeat q5 min if signs persist (max 10 mg/kg). Works by blocking calcium release in muscle cells. (7) Ice packs (option D) are supportive for later cooling but NOT the first action—stopping triggers is. (8) Additional muscle relaxant (option C) would worsen MH. (9) This teaches the critical lesson: Recognize EARLY signs (muscle rigidity, tachycardia, rising ETCO2) BEFORE fever appears. Option B is correct.`,
