    rationale: `This tests understanding of MH vs. NON-MH causes of ETCO2 elevation and RISK STRATIFICATION. Clinical reasoning: (1) ETCO2 can rise for many reasons: Inadequate ventilation, increased CO2 production (fever, exercise, malignant hyperthermia, hypermetabolism), CO2 insufflation (laparoscopy). (2) This patient: Laparoscopic surgery = CO2 insufflated for visualization. That alone causes SOME ETCO2 elevation. (3) Anesthetic agents: (a) Propofol (induction)—NON-triggering agent for MH. (b) Rocuronium—NON-depolarizing muscle relaxant (safe for MH patients). These are NOT triggers. (c) She did NOT receive sevoflurane or succinylcholine (triggers). (4) Clinical picture: (a) Mild ETCO2 elevation (48, not 60+). (b) HR stable (95). (c) BP stable. (d) Temp normal (37.2°C)—NO fever. (e) No muscle rigidity mentioned. (f) Surgical oozing normal (not excessive). (5) When ETCO2 elevated and ventilation increased, it NORMALIZED—classic sign of ventilation-limited ETCO2, not hypermetabolic cause. In true MH, increasing ventilation might temporarily help but other signs would persist (muscle rigidity, continued temp rise, continued HR rise). (6) Diagnosis: Inadequate ventilation or laparoscopic CO2 insufflation causing mild hypercapnia. Ventilation adjusted → problem solved. (7) This teaches discrimination: Not every ETCO2 elevation = MH. Must look at FULL CLINICAL PICTURE. (8) MH red flags absent here: Non-triggering drugs, stable vitals, normal temp, no muscle rigidity. (9) Option B is correct and teaches clinical pattern recognition.`,
