    rationale: `This tests understanding of MH RECRUDESCENCE and late-phase MH presentations. Clinical reasoning: (1) Malignant hyperthermia is typically recognized DURING surgery (minutes to hours from trigger exposure). (2) However, in ~3-5% of MH cases, DELAYED MH ("recrudescence") can occur AFTER initial anesthetic exposure, even HOURS later, even when non-triggering agents used. (3) Mechanism: Calcium dysregulation in muscle can persist post-operatively in susceptible patients. Stress (surgery, ICU) can trigger late MH symptoms. (4) Presentation in this patient: (a) Fever—NOW appearing (was normal at 6 hours, then SUDDEN onset). (b) Muscle rigidity—trunk and chest. (c) Tachycardia—response to hypermetabolism. (d) Elevated ETCO2—hypermetabolism. (e) Rising CPK—muscle breakdown (rhabdomyolysis). (5) Why recrudescence is possible here: Even though surgery used non-triggering drugs, the patient's GENETIC SUSCEPTIBILITY means calcium-handling abnormality existed during surgery. Small accumulation of calcium dysregulation could trigger post-op MH. (6) Management: (a) CONTINUE dantrolene: 1 mg/kg IV q4-6h × 24-48 hours (prevents recurrence). (b) Aggressive cooling: ICE, cooling blankets, chilled IV fluids. (c) Hyperventilation. (d) Continuous monitoring. (e) Labs: Repeat CPK, electrolytes (K+ likely rising with rhabdomyolysis), ABG, urine myoglobin. (f) Aggressive hydration to prevent myoglobin-induced AKI. (g) Monitor urine color (dark/cola if myoglobinuria). (7) Sepsis (option A): Could present with fever, tachycardia, but would NOT cause muscle rigidity and rising CPK acutely. Sepsis develops over hours-days, not suddenly. (8) Normal post-op fever (option C): Post-op fever is common but usually mild and gradual. Sudden fever + muscle rigidity + rising CPK is NOT typical post-op response. (9) Option D (impossible with non-triggering drugs): WRONG—recrudescence can occur even with non-triggering anesthesia. (10) Study guide emphasizes MH recognition. This teaches that MH doesn't END at extubation—vigilance continues. Option B is correct.`,
