    rationale: `This tests understanding of POST-OPERATIVE MANAGEMENT after MH crisis and identification of rhabdomyolysis complications. Clinical reasoning: (1) Malignant hyperthermia causes: Uncontrolled muscle contraction → muscle tissue breakdown (rhabdomyolysis) → release of myoglobin into bloodstream. (2) This patient: Massively elevated CPK (8500, normal <200)—indicates significant muscle breakdown. Dark urine—myoglobinuria (myoglobin in urine). Elevated creatinine—early kidney dysfunction. (3) Rhabdomyolysis mechanism of kidney injury: (a) Myoglobin filtered into urine. (b) In acidic urine → myoglobin precipitates in renal tubules. (c) Precipitation → tubular obstruction and direct toxicity. (d) Results in acute kidney injury (AKI) / acute tubular necrosis (ATN). (4) Management PRIORITIES: (a) AGGRESSIVELY HYDRATE: IV fluids to increase urine output (goal >1 mL/kg/hr, some say >200-300 mL/hr for rhabdomyolysis cases). (b) ALKALINIZE URINE: Sodium bicarbonate IV to maintain urine pH >6.5. In alkaline urine, myoglobin stays soluble and doesn't precipitate. (c) MONITOR: Urine output (color, volume), Creatinine (trend), CPK (trend down). (d) AVOID NEPHROTOXINS: No NSAIDs, aminoglycosides (as seen in earlier topics), contrast. (e) PREPARE for dialysis if renal function continues deteriorating. (5) Why this is priority #1: (a) Kidney failure can develop over hours. (b) Hyperkalemia (K+ 5.8) is also a concern with rhabdomyolysis and must be managed (calcium gluconate if peaked T waves, insulin/glucose, bicarbonate). (c) Once kidneys fail, long-term morbidity/mortality increases. (6) Other issues present: (a) Dantrolene—CONTINUE (not discontinue option D). It's preventing MH recrudescence. Takes time to metabolize. (b) Respiratory—on ventilator, stable. Muscle paralysis wore off (reversal agents given). (c) Infection—low risk acutely, though ICU protocols followed. (7) Acidosis (pH 7.28): Contribute to AKI risk. IV bicarbonate helps both kidney function and hyperkalemia. (8) Option A (respiratory failure): Patient is on ventilator, stable. Not acute priority. (9) Option C (infection): No fever, no signs of infection yet. (10) Option B is correct and prioritizes the life-threatening AKI risk from rhabdomyolysis.`,
