    rationale: `This tests understanding of MH GENETIC INHERITANCE and patient education/family counseling. Clinical reasoning: (1) Malignant hyperthermia inheritance: Autosomal dominant, with incomplete penetrance. If parent has MH gene, each child has ~50% chance of inheriting it. (2) This patient's situation: (a) Father has family history (mother died of MH—likely had MH gene). (b) Father is at LEAST a carrier (50% chance of inheriting from his mother). (c) Father has HAD anesthesia twice without MH manifestation. This doesn't mean he doesn't carry the gene—incomplete penetrance means some carriers DON'T manifest MH. (d) Whether father manifests or not, if he inherited the gene, he can pass it to his children. (3) Genetic risk to son: (a) SON has 50% chance of inheriting MH gene from FATHER. (b) If son inherits gene and receives triggering anesthetics, he's at HIGH risk of MH. (c) Son's prior anesthesia (if any) without MH doesn't rule out susceptibility. (d) Genetic testing: Available at specialized centers (caffeine halothane contracture test, genetic testing for RYR1/CACNA1S mutations). (4) Son's management: (a) MUST inform his anesthesiologist of family history BEFORE knee surgery. (b) Pre-operative assessment should include specific MH risk questions. (c) Anesthesia plan: MH-safe (non-triggering agents). (d) Genetic testing PRIOR to surgery ideal (if available). (e) MedAlert bracelet for life. (f) Family members should be informed of inheritance risk. (5) This teaches critical genetic counseling concept: Family screening is essential. One member's experience (or lack thereof) doesn't predict another's risk. (6) Option A (no counseling): WRONG—son needs to know his risk. (7) Option B (avoid all surgery): Too restrictive. Surgery can proceed with MH-safe anesthesia. (8) Option D (only siblings affected): WRONG—autosomal dominant means parent-to-child transmission. (9) Option C is correct and emphasizes family genetic counseling.`,
