    rationale: `This tests understanding of comprehensive MH patient EDUCATION and communication strategies. Clinical reasoning: (1) Post-MH crisis education must be thorough and practical. Patient must understand lifelong implications. (2) MedAlert bracelet: GOOD, but NOT sufficient. Many providers don't check bracelets. Many providers don't understand MH or what it means. (3) Written summary: Patient should receive: (a) List of SAFE anesthetics (propofol, non-depolarizing muscle relaxants, non-volatile agents). (b) List of UNSAFE agents (all volatile anesthetics, succinylcholine). (c) MH-specific precautions for future anesthesia. (d) Emergency contact number (MHAUS—Malignant Hyperthermia Association 1-800-986-MHAUS). (e) Dantrolene availability instructions. (4) Why all providers matter: (a) DENTISTS: Often use local anesthesia + sedation. If IV sedation needed, dentist must use non-triggering agents. (b) ERs: If trauma/emergency surgery, ER staff must know about MH. (c) OTHER SURGEONS: Of course. (d) ROUTINE PROCEDURES: Even colonoscopy, biopsies, etc. can require anesthesia. (5) Family screening: (a) MH is autosomal dominant. Family members (siblings, children, parents) may be susceptible. (b) Genetic testing should be offered/encouraged. (c) Family members should receive education about their risk. (6) Discharge summary should include: (a) MH diagnosis/susceptibility confirmed. (b) Anesthetic plan used (non-triggering agents). (c) Safe agents for future. (d) Unsafe agents to avoid. (e) Instructions to carry this with them. (f) Contact MHAUS for resources. (7) Option C (bracelet only): Insufficient. Bracelets can be missed, misunderstood. (8) Option D (only surgeons): WRONG. Dentists absolutely need to know. (9) Option A (education complete): WRONG if comprehensive communication strategy not detailed. (10) Option B is correct and emphasizes the breadth of MH patient education beyond initial crisis management.`,
