    rationale: `This tests understanding of MULTIPLE ALLERGY/COMPLICATION MANAGEMENT simultaneously in acute perioperative setting. Clinical reasoning: (1) This patient has TWO significant risks: (a) MH susceptibility (family history, no testing). (b) Latex allergy (anaphylaxis risk). (2) MH management: Non-triggering anesthesia. Dantrolene available. Monitoring. (3) Latex allergy management: Latex-free protocol. Latex-free gloves, equipment, tubing. (4) The interaction: If latex-free protocol is done WRONG (expired stock, cross-contamination, etc.), patient could have anaphylaxis during induction. (5) Anaphylaxis during induction is a CRISIS on top of MH risk: (a) Anaphylaxis causes tachycardia, hypertension, bronchospasm, hypoxia. (b) These signs mimic EARLY MH (tachycardia, hypertension). (c) Could confuse MH diagnosis. (d) Management differs: Anaphylaxis needs epinephrine, airway support. MH needs dantrolene. (e) Dual management possible but complex. (6) Prevention: VERIFY latex-free precautions. (a) Check all equipment is truly latex-free. (b) Use latex-free kits specifically prepared. (c) Inspect for expired or contaminated items. (d) Brief OR staff on dual precautions (MH + latex). (e) Have emergency medication ready for both MH (dantrolene) and anaphylaxis (epinephrine, IM). (7) Pre-medication: Antihistamines/corticosteroids sometimes given for latex allergy if time permits, but in 30-minute emergency window, may not be possible. (8) Study guide doesn't specifically address MH + latex-allergy intersection, but this tests advanced clinical thinking. (9) Option A (unrelated): WRONG—they interact in management complexity. (10) Option C (focus only on latex): WRONG—both risks need attention. (11) Option D (pre-medication prevents anaphylaxis): WRONG—pre-medication can reduce risk but not eliminate it. (12) Option B is correct and shows understanding of multi-system risk management.`,
