    q: `A 35-year-old male with family history of malignant hyperthermia (father had MH crisis successfully treated 15 years ago) is scheduled for elective appendectomy. He is anxious about surgery. Pre-operative assessment is thorough. The anesthesia team implements MH-safe protocol: propofol induction, rocuronium, NO volatile anesthetics. Intra-operatively, at 45 minutes, the patient develops: TACHYCARDIA (HR 125), TACHYPNEA (RR 30), muscle rigidity noted by surgical staff, and RISING ETCO2 (72 mmHg). Dantrolene is prepared and administered 2.5 mg/kg IV. After 10 minutes, vitals begin to normalize. Dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg is repeated once more (total 5 mg/kg given). The team is CONFIDENT the MH crisis is controlled. However, which factors regarding this patient's care STILL REQUIRE ATTENTION? (SELECT ALL THAT APPLY)`,
